---
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import AppShell from "@components/app/AppShell.astro";
import AppProse from "@components/app/Prose.astro";

const title = "The Trade Desk Alternatives (2026): 5 DSPs Compared";
const description = "The best Trade Desk alternatives: Google DV360, Amazon DSP, Basis, Criteo and Quantcast, with who each suits, how each is priced, and when to stay with The Trade Desk.";
const slug = "the-trade-desk/alternatives";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What is the best alternative to The Trade Desk?",
    a: "Google DV360 is the most direct competitor for enterprise programmatic buying, and the only one of the two with exclusive YouTube inventory. Amazon DSP is the choice when Amazon's shopping data or Prime Video inventory matters. Basis suits agencies that want buying and campaign operations in one system, Criteo suits retail and retargeting goals, and Quantcast is the most accessible self-serve option.",
  },
  {
    q: "The Trade Desk vs DV360 vs Amazon DSP: which is best for programmatic buying?",
    a: "The Trade Desk for independent buying across the open internet, with no owned media to favor. DV360 when YouTube and the Google Marketing Platform are central to the plan. Amazon DSP when Amazon's first-party shopping data or its owned inventory (Prime Video, Twitch, Thursday Night Football) is the reason to buy. Many large advertisers run two or all three.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is there a cheaper alternative to The Trade Desk?",
    a: "Amazon DSP's self-service option has no minimum spend (you pay media plus a technology fee). Quantcast's self-serve platform has no traditional long-term contracts or minimums for basic entry. Basis doesn't publish pricing but its profile notes it does not impose the high minimums associated with tier-one DSPs. The Trade Desk's own fees are estimated at about 15–20% of media spend.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is DV360 better than The Trade Desk?",
    a: "Neither is better across the board. DV360's advantage is exclusive YouTube and Google Display Network inventory and tight Google stack integration. The Trade Desk's advantage is independence: it owns no media, so its optimization isn't tied to any inventory source. Most large agencies maintain both.",
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} slug={slug} faqSchema={faqSchema}>
  <AppShell>
    <div class="max-w-3xl w-full mx-auto py-12 px-4">
      <AppProse>

<p><em>Last reviewed November 2026.</em></p>

<h1>The Trade Desk Alternatives: 5 DSPs Compared</h1>

<p>Teams look for Trade Desk alternatives when they need inventory The Trade Desk can't reach (YouTube, Amazon's owned properties), a different kind of data (retail purchase signals), more of the media workflow in one place, or a lower barrier to entry. The Trade Desk itself is the leading independent demand-side platform, with the Kokai AI platform, Unified ID 2.0 and access to 80+ exchanges across CTV, display, video, audio and digital out-of-home. It doesn't publish pricing; fees are estimated at about 15–20% of media spend including data and measurement. See <a href="/the-trade-desk/pricing">The Trade Desk pricing</a> for the full fee breakdown.</p>

<h2>Quick comparison</h2>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Alternative</th><th>Best for</th><th>Pricing</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><a href="/google-dv360">Google DV360</a></td><td>Enterprise programmatic with YouTube and the Google stack</td><td>Contract; about 10–15% of open-auction spend, about 4% on YouTube and programmatic guaranteed</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/amazon-dsp">Amazon DSP</a></td><td>Targeting with Amazon's first-party shopping data</td><td>Self-service: no minimum, technology fee on spend. Managed service: $50K/month minimum</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/basis">Basis</a></td><td>Agencies running buying, search, social and direct buys in one system</td><td>Enterprise, custom quote</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/criteo">Criteo</a></td><td>Retail media, commerce data and retargeting</td><td>CPC or CPM, platform fee as a % of spend, no setup fee</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/quantcast">Quantcast</a></td><td>Self-serve, AI-optimized open-web buying</td><td>Self-serve with no minimums for basic entry; managed service custom</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Google DV360</h2>
<p>Google Display &amp; Video 360 is Google's enterprise DSP: planning, buying, creative and measurement in one platform, with access to 90+ exchanges and YouTube and Google Display Network inventory that other DSPs can't buy. It integrates natively with Google Ads, Analytics 360 and Campaign Manager 360. It isn't self-serve; access needs a Google Marketing Platform contract, and $50,000+ a month in media is typically the practical threshold.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it over The Trade Desk when:</strong> YouTube is a core part of the plan, or your measurement and audiences already live in the Google Marketing Platform.</p>
<p><strong>Stay with The Trade Desk when:</strong> you want an independent platform with no owned media, broader data marketplace access and supply-path transparency across the open internet.</p>
<p><a href="/google-dv360">Google DV360 profile →</a></p>

<h2>Amazon DSP</h2>
<p>Amazon DSP buys Amazon's owned properties (Prime Video, Twitch, Thursday Night Football, Freevee, Kindle and Alexa devices) alongside third-party publishers, and targets with Amazon's first-party shopping, browsing and streaming data. Self-service has no minimum spend; managed service requires $50,000 a month.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it over The Trade Desk when:</strong> Amazon's purchase data or its streaming inventory is the reason for the buy, typically for retail and CPG brands.</p>
<p><strong>Stay with The Trade Desk when:</strong> you need a broader open-internet footprint with more publisher choice. Many enterprise advertisers run both for complementary audiences.</p>
<p><a href="/amazon-dsp">Amazon DSP profile →</a></p>

<h2>Basis</h2>
<p>Basis (formerly Centro) runs a DSP alongside a campaign management suite that covers paid search, social, direct publisher buys and financial reconciliation. Its DSP has ranked No. 1 in G2's demand-side platform category every quarter since 2017. Pricing is custom with no self-serve tier, and its profile notes it doesn't impose the high minimums associated with tier-one DSPs.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it over The Trade Desk when:</strong> the agency needs planning, buying, reporting and billing in one workflow rather than a standalone DSP, or budgets are smaller.</p>
<p><strong>Stay with The Trade Desk when:</strong> DSP performance and the depth of programmatic optimization matter most.</p>
<p><a href="/basis">Basis profile →</a></p>

<h2>Criteo</h2>
<p>Criteo is a performance marketing and retail media platform built on commerce data, covering retargeting, customer acquisition and retail media. It charges on a CPC or CPM basis with a platform fee as a percentage of media spend, and has no setup fee.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it over The Trade Desk when:</strong> the goals are e-commerce sales, retargeting or retail media network access.</p>
<p><strong>Stay with The Trade Desk when:</strong> the plan is about prospecting and brand reach across the open internet.</p>
<p><a href="/criteo">Criteo profile →</a></p>

<h2>Quantcast</h2>
<p>Quantcast is an AI-powered programmatic platform for open-web display, video, CTV, mobile and audio, using its audience graph and predictive modeling to find likely converters. The self-serve platform has no traditional long-term contracts or minimums for basic entry; managed service and enterprise packages are custom.</p>
<p><strong>Choose it over The Trade Desk when:</strong> you want a lower barrier to entry and self-serve, AI-driven audience targeting.</p>
<p><strong>Stay with The Trade Desk when:</strong> you need enterprise scale, deal management for upfronts and guaranteed buys (Deal Desk), and the widest exchange and data partner coverage.</p>
<p><a href="/quantcast">Quantcast profile →</a></p>

<h2>How to choose</h2>
<p>Start with the inventory and data you can't do without. YouTube points to DV360. Amazon's shopping data or Prime Video points to Amazon DSP. Retail media and retargeting point to Criteo. If the problem is operational (too many systems for buying, reporting and billing), look at Basis. If the problem is access, Quantcast's self-serve platform is the easiest way in. The Trade Desk remains the default for independent, open-internet programmatic at scale, and many large advertisers pair it with DV360 or Amazon DSP rather than replacing it.</p>

